# Netlify reads this file from the publish root and applies it ahead of the
# [[redirects]] blocks in netlify.toml.
#
# It exists because those netlify.toml rules are NOT reaching production.
# Measured on the live site:
#   /projects            -> 200 (should be 301 to /portfolio)
#   /work                -> 200 (should be 301 to /portfolio)
#   /any-unknown-url     -> 200, serving 85KB of Angular app shell,
#                           not the 2.6KB public/404.html
# Something is answering every path before netlify.toml is consulted - most
# likely Netlify's auto-detected Angular runtime, or "Pretty URLs" still
# enabled in the dashboard (Site settings -> Build & deploy -> Post
# processing). Check both there; this file is the in-repo defence.

# Force (!) so these win even against a catch-all that claims the path.
/projects    /portfolio    301!
/work        /portfolio    301!

# Real 404 status for anything with no matching file. Safe for the 58
# prerendered routes: Netlify always serves an existing static file before it
# evaluates a non-forced redirect rule, and every route (including all 49
# /blog/:slug articles) is its own prerendered index.html.
/*           /404.html     404
